 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A∼B,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{3}=0,$ where ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variables. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-111478/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cba14b5-62f7-5fbe-beb1-9b1511305cc6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-111478"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-34523/selection/7c2dedc7452278d787a5a2224a548c32ebe5b1641536cd1a6f0bc365db0c4930" :answer/id #uuid "545b5ddb-6502-553e-897a-def076ce767c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-34523/selection/fbf02a4936de1b58d75712392f899f5e5131a59b421261eb5ebe0bc2f3e63e13" :answer/id #uuid "0c75e83b-3827-5d2e-8b6e-e281b42b52e1" :a